1
00:00:02,179 --> 00:00:04,277
We spent quite some time on that import, but

2
00:00:04,293 --> 00:00:06,937
it is important that you understand how the

3
00:00:06,937 --> 00:00:09,853
standard library works and how you import

4
00:00:09,869 --> 00:00:10,734
features from it.

5
00:00:11,391 --> 00:00:13,234
With serve being imported, we can now

6
00:00:13,314 --> 00:00:16,022
execute it because it's a function to spin

7
00:00:16,118 --> 00:00:16,775
up a server.

8
00:00:17,319 --> 00:00:19,258
Now at the moment, I'm getting no help by my

9
00:00:19,258 --> 00:00:21,790
IDE here. The reason for that should be that

10
00:00:21,886 --> 00:00:24,610
we haven't run this code once, and therefore

11
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:27,109
this package, which is on some web server,

12
00:00:27,413 --> 00:00:30,283
hasn't been downloaded locally yet. That is

13
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:32,704
something which Deno will do eventually, but

14
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,805
it hasn't done it yet, and therefore my IDE

15
00:00:34,965 --> 00:00:37,307
can't look into this package. It doesn't

16
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:39,359
reach out to that server, and therefore it

17
00:00:39,359 --> 00:00:40,514
doesn't know what's in there.

18
00:00:41,219 --> 00:00:43,673
Nonetheless, I can tell you serve takes an

19
00:00:43,849 --> 00:00:46,271
object to be configured so that we can

20
00:00:46,271 --> 00:00:47,313
configure the server,

21
00:00:47,874 --> 00:00:50,328
and there we can configure the port on which

22
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:52,525
we want to listen. Now for development, we

23
00:00:52,589 --> 00:00:56,053
want to use something like 8000 or 3000.

24
00:00:56,437 --> 00:00:59,098
These are common development ports since

25
00:00:59,098 --> 00:01:01,311
they're not occupied by other processes.

26
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:04,308
Now this will return us a server,

27
00:01:05,046 --> 00:01:07,274
so I'll store it in a constant named server.

28
00:01:07,819 --> 00:01:10,079
It's a synchronous process, so we don't need

29
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:10,448
await.

30
00:01:11,121 --> 00:01:14,343
But with that, we can now react to incoming

31
00:01:14,407 --> 00:01:15,016
requests.

32
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:18,126
And the obvious question is, how does this

33
00:01:18,158 --> 00:01:18,479
work?

34
00:01:19,216 --> 00:01:19,329
Well,

35
00:01:19,938 --> 00:01:22,503
this server thing here actually

36
00:01:23,049 --> 00:01:25,313
is an asynchronous iterable,

37
00:01:25,939 --> 00:01:28,589
and we had a look at those in the JavaScript

38
00:01:28,605 --> 00:01:31,431
and TypeScript refresher section. So if you

39
00:01:31,431 --> 00:01:34,112
skip this, in that section you can learn

40
00:01:34,144 --> 00:01:35,911
what async iterables are.

41
00:01:36,473 --> 00:01:38,480
In the end, it's like a never-ending array,

42
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,177
you could say, or potentially never-ending.

43
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:43,907
It could end, but it's like an array full of

44
00:01:44,083 --> 00:01:46,941
asynchronous actions, full of promises in

45
00:01:46,990 --> 00:01:47,327
the end.

46
00:01:48,338 --> 00:01:50,681
And it's not really an array, it's an object

47
00:01:50,681 --> 00:01:52,655
that instead continues emitting new

48
00:01:52,848 --> 00:01:55,801
promises. And we can loop through this kind

49
00:01:55,801 --> 00:01:57,470
of stream of promises

50
00:01:58,209 --> 00:02:02,687
with for-of, but with for-await-of.

51
00:02:03,361 --> 00:02:05,415
That's a special JavaScript syntax which

52
00:02:05,463 --> 00:02:07,983
exists in JavaScript and which is supported

53
00:02:08,015 --> 00:02:08,417
by Deno.

54
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:09,990
We can

55
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:13,841
loop through all requests of server. This is

56
00:02:14,177 --> 00:02:17,736
how you react to incoming requests in Deno.

57
00:02:18,457 --> 00:02:20,397
Now that's quite different to what we do

58
00:02:20,509 --> 00:02:23,090
with Node, and I will come back to Node in a

59
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:25,639
second so that we can have a side-by-side

60
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:28,220
comparison. For the moment, let's just add

61
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,865
this code and this will basically loop

62
00:02:31,089 --> 00:02:33,830
through all incoming requests and whenever a

63
00:02:33,846 --> 00:02:36,523
new request is incoming, the loop body will

64
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:37,229
execute again.

65
00:02:37,774 --> 00:02:40,274
So this is not kind of collecting all

66
00:02:40,323 --> 00:02:42,134
requests and then looping through all of

67
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:44,827
them at once. Instead, it will really fire

68
00:02:44,971 --> 00:02:47,698
that loop body for every incoming request

69
00:02:47,938 --> 00:02:49,109
when it's incoming.

70
00:02:50,504 --> 00:02:52,493
So therefore, in here we can react to the

71
00:02:52,493 --> 00:02:55,027
request. And a simple reaction right now

72
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,667
is that on the request object, we call the

73
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:01,810
respond method. And respond does what the

74
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:04,457
name implies— it sends back a response.

75
00:03:05,339 --> 00:03:07,824
Now, which kind of response? Well, it wants

76
00:03:07,824 --> 00:03:09,861
an object here, and in that object we can

77
00:03:09,909 --> 00:03:10,599
set a body

78
00:03:11,257 --> 00:03:13,376
And that should be some text. And here

79
00:03:14,388 --> 00:03:16,973
I will just say response from Deno.

80
00:03:17,695 --> 00:03:19,542
You can of course add any text you want.

81
00:03:20,023 --> 00:03:22,014
This is the text with which we're going to

82
00:03:22,014 --> 00:03:22,849
respond for now.

83
00:03:23,475 --> 00:03:25,771
And if you now save this, you can execute

84
00:03:25,948 --> 00:03:27,313
this first web server,

85
00:03:27,971 --> 00:03:28,212
deno

86
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:29,625
run

87
00:03:30,379 --> 00:03:33,205
first-web-server.ts. Now this will crash,

88
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:36,047
as you will see in a second,

89
00:03:36,641 --> 00:03:39,772
but what it does first is it downloads the

90
00:03:39,772 --> 00:03:42,902
standard library package, or the standard

91
00:03:42,902 --> 00:03:45,149
library module as we should call it,

92
00:03:45,775 --> 00:03:47,204
and caches it locally.

93
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:50,173
So first, of course, it compiled the code,

94
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:52,180
but then it downloaded server.ts,

95
00:03:52,998 --> 00:03:55,149
and it also downloaded other files from the

96
00:03:55,149 --> 00:03:57,461
standard library because the server module

97
00:03:57,589 --> 00:03:59,724
does actually use other modules from the

98
00:03:59,724 --> 00:04:02,132
standard library, and those other modules

99
00:04:02,164 --> 00:04:04,989
might then again use other modules. So now

100
00:04:05,278 --> 00:04:08,422
Deno downloaded all those modules that are

101
00:04:08,422 --> 00:04:11,434
required by us or by the modules our module

102
00:04:11,627 --> 00:04:12,716
requires implicitly,

103
00:04:13,309 --> 00:04:15,440
and it downloads them locally and caches

104
00:04:15,472 --> 00:04:15,824
them here

105
00:04:16,353 --> 00:04:18,164
so that we don't have to wait for all those

106
00:04:18,196 --> 00:04:20,615
downloads to finish every time we run our

107
00:04:20,615 --> 00:04:21,160
script.

108
00:04:21,704 --> 00:04:23,771
This also means that if you're on a plane,

109
00:04:24,188 --> 00:04:26,222
you just need to run it once before you

110
00:04:26,287 --> 00:04:28,578
board the plane, and then you can continue

111
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:30,933
developing on the plane because you don't

112
00:04:30,933 --> 00:04:32,134
need to re-download it.

113
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:34,858
If you for some reason would want to

114
00:04:34,858 --> 00:04:37,871
re-download it, you can always run your code

115
00:04:38,466 --> 00:04:38,787
with the

116
00:04:39,590 --> 00:04:40,393
--reload flag.

117
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:43,478
This then forces the downloads to run again.

118
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:46,722
If you don't do that, if you don't add

119
00:04:46,899 --> 00:04:49,292
--reload, which typically is the default— so

120
00:04:49,533 --> 00:04:51,638
not adding it is the default, I mean— you

121
00:04:51,638 --> 00:04:53,357
see it's not getting downloaded again.

122
00:04:54,369 --> 00:04:56,055
By the way, it's also not getting compiled

123
00:04:56,055 --> 00:04:58,336
again unless you change the code, just to

124
00:04:58,384 --> 00:04:58,979
speed this up.

125
00:04:59,589 --> 00:05:01,485
And with that, this is all generally

126
00:05:01,661 --> 00:05:01,967
working,

127
00:05:02,561 --> 00:05:04,456
but of course we got a permission denied

128
00:05:04,488 --> 00:05:05,002
error here.

129
00:05:05,614 --> 00:05:07,772
And that should make sense because we're

130
00:05:07,772 --> 00:05:09,350
trying to use the network here.

131
00:05:09,881 --> 00:05:11,862
We're not sending a request, of course, as

132
00:05:11,959 --> 00:05:12,490
we did before,

133
00:05:13,183 --> 00:05:15,196
but we're trying to create a server that

134
00:05:15,196 --> 00:05:17,740
should handle incoming requests and that of

135
00:05:17,837 --> 00:05:19,157
course uses the network.

136
00:05:19,785 --> 00:05:21,637
And therefore we need to add the protocol.
